Tests, run on the system itself, in two modes.

The core is a set of regression tests which together finish in well
under 10 seconds, on either machine, and is always run under a strict
10 second limit: on the board 10 seconds of time, and on QEMU 10
seconds of the CPU time of the emulator, which a busy host does not
change. Running out of time is a failure. Boot the system and type

	/usr/local/test/run.sh		all core tests
	/usr/local/test/run.sh k12 kern	only the named tests

run.sh prints PASS or FAIL for each test, and finally a line
"run.sh: N passed, M failed".

The fuzzers run for ever, trying random combinations of operations and
checking each result, until they are stopped. Type

	sh /usr/local/test/fuzz.sh [seed]

Each round prints a line "fuzz round N seeds S-T done", and any
failure a line beginning FAIL, with the seed which repeats it. A
round runs three copies of fzfs, which makes random file system calls
on a few names and checks each against a model of the files; fzproc,
which builds random families of processes joined by pipes, kills them
or closes their pipes at random points, and checks the data and the
statuses wait returns; and fzsh, which computes the same thing in two
ways with the commands on random data from fzgen. The root file system
is then checked with icheck and dcheck. To repeat a failure, run the
fuzzer named, with the seed printed, in a directory of its own.

A core test is cases/NAME, a shell script; cases/NAME.exp, its
expected output, standard output and standard error together; and
optionally cases/NAME.sed, which normalises output that legitimately
varies, such as times, clock ticks, process ids, and kernel addresses.
run.sh runs the script with sh, from the cases directory, passes its
output through the .sed, and compares the result with the .exp. Once
cmp has found a difference, the rest of the output is read and thrown
away, so that a failing test has finished before the next one starts.
A test passes when cmp prints nothing; the exit status is not used,
since sh gives a pipeline the first nonzero status it reaps, which
depends on timing. To see why a test fails, run it by hand:

	cd /usr/local/test/cases; sh NAME

utils holds the programs the tests and fuzzers use, compiled from
utils/*.c by the Makefile (test.mk), and fzsh, a shell script.

Each test works in a directory of its own, /tmp/NAME, which it removes
at the end, and the fuzzers in /tmp/fz. A test which changes anything
else puts it back before it ends, and kills every process it started,
so that no test depends on which tests ran before it. Its output must
not depend on timing, and no test waits a fixed time for another
process. A test which interrupts a command starts it through
utils/pidf, which writes its own process id to a file and then
executes the command; the process which sends the signal waits until
the file exists.
